        "outputsReviewed": 12,
        "findings": [
          "The negative-diagonal candidate loses several clear sun rays compared with the current policy in both repetitions. The regression is visible, not only a numerical score difference.",
          "Vertical rays are fuller in the candidate, especially the second repetition, although local irregularities and border bends remain.",
          "Both stacked reconstructions retain thin false seams and imperfect joins. The faster backend does not establish better motif recovery. This fixture is a photographic appearance target, not proof of the publisher's original paths."
        ]
      },
      "decision": "Not integrated: stacked and negative-diagonal image errors worsen in both repetitions. Faster complete audits do not establish better finite-deadline fitting."
    }
  },
  "findings": [
    "Checkpoint processing consumed 57.5\u201373.7% of refinement time in the first three diagnostic runs. Fully fixed visible edges accounted for at most 11 of 147, so skipping their gradients would address little of the measured cost.",
    "The second instrumentation separates geometry, paper, scoring and repairs. First-time finer paper checks cost about 491 and 707 scaled milliseconds in the negative-diagonal and stacked cases.",
    "An isolated 1000-pixel lattice audit took 660 ms before calibration and about 153 ms once calibrated. The first audit repeats JavaScript alongside WebAssembly to select kernels for the new size.",
    "A prototype that uses the available native paper kernels directly reduces first-audit cost across all seven saved geometries in Node and Chromium. Existing native input validation and JavaScript near-threshold distance handling stay in place.",
    "All 336 timed complete audits agree with the JavaScript oracle in every non-time field and map pixel. Retained arrays are checked after later calls.",
    "Instrumentation changes elapsed-time decisions. The second vertical replay has 1.559375% mask disagreement but fails the final offset-radius probes: a 0.251744 mm\u00b2 island just exceeds the 0.25 mm\u00b2 significance threshold at one probe. No validity rule is relaxed."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Seven geometries and one local machine do not establish universal speed or complete solver quality.",
    "The profiling clocks for near-miss cases are scaled by 1.5; proportions are useful, raw durations are not direct wall-clock measurements.",
    "Profiling snapshots are audit inputs only, not initialization templates for later fitting.",
    "The native-first prototype removes runtime paired equality calibration; extensive kernel regression coverage and browser replay are required before considering integration.",
    "Full finite-deadline solves can change selected geometry even when each audit is identical. The running comparison retains these outcomes rather than treating microbenchmark speed as a release gate."
  ],
